diagnosis_text:
No fault exists in this network. The helpdesk ticket's claim that the WAN uplink between the routers carrying traffic toward 128.168.0.0/16 is down is incorrect.

Every component of the alleged failure path was inspected directly and found healthy. The wan_r1_r2 segment (router1.eth3 = 20.20.0.1/8, router2.eth2 = 20.20.0.2/8) is fully UP with LOWER_UP carrier on both endpoints. RIP version 2 is correctly configured on all three routers: router1 advertises its four connected subnets (192.168.3.0/24, 192.168.4.0/24, 10.0.0.0/8, 20.0.0.0/8) and router2 advertises 1.0.0.0/8, 128.168.0.0/16, and 20.0.0.0/8. Both routers have fully populated FIBs: router1 holds an active RIP route to 128.168.0.0/16 via 20.20.0.2 (metric 2), and router2 holds a return route to 192.168.3.0/24 via 20.20.0.1 (metric 2). End-host default gateways are correctly set (office_pc → 192.168.3.1, pc0 → 192.168.4.1, pc2 → 128.168.0.1). Live pings from both the Office LAN (office_pc) and the Principle LAN (pc0) to Internet Lab hosts (128.168.0.2, 128.168.0.3) complete with 0% packet loss, as does the full automated reachability sweep across all 12 tested host pairs. The root cause of the ticket is a false premise: the reported outage does not correspond to any actual network fault. No corrective action is needed; the network is operating normally and the helpdesk ticket should be closed as a false alarm.
=== END ===